			"Message": "fix(Output): Add missing diagnostics stub methods\n\nAdd `getWorkspaceFileExtensions` and fix `reportWorkspaceStats` stub shapes in TauriMainProcessService diagnostics channel.\n\nThe `languageDetectionWorker ServiceImpl.resolveWorkspaceLanguageIds` iterates `fileExtensions.extensions` directly and throws without this stub, causing renderer crashes when language detection runs. Also fixes `reportWorkspaceStats` from `undefined` to a proper object shape with configFiles, fileTypes, and launchConfigFiles arrays.\n\nThis ensures the diagnostics IPC channel returns valid shapes that VS Code's services expect.",
			"SHA": "8131d76472e855192ba38169571d6c956eddc6b4"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Auto",
			"Date": "2026-04-23T03:23:58Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				218,
				1
			],
			"Message": "",
			"SHA": "1000c0465fcaa11d972f4f9fc0af24698d9cc6e4"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-04-23T01:45:59Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				218,
				6
			],
			"Message": "feat(Output): Add StripWebviewIframeSandbox transform plugin\n\nAdd Step 16 to the build pipeline that removes the sandbox attribute from VS Code's webview iframe. WKWebView (Tauri on macOS) blocks custom protocol loads (`vscode-webview://`, `land://`) from sandboxed iframes, causing webview panels in extensions to fail with:\n\n  [Error] Ignoring request to load this main resource because it has a custom protocol and comes from a sandboxed iframe (x12)\n\nThe plugin patches `workbench/contrib/webview/browser/pre/index.html` to remove the `setAttribute('sandbox', ...)` call. This is safe for Land because it runs as a single-user desktop editor where the upstream sandbox's threat model (untrusted extension HTML in a browser tab) does not apply.",
			"SHA": "868f077360f8c7bb3c8af52b2899a1f8631b9ab4"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-04-23T01:28:21Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				215,
				3
			],
			"Message": "fix(Output): Handle ILocalExtension envelope shape in extension scanner\n\nMountain's scanSystem/UserExtensions now returns ILocalExtension-wrapped objects with nested manifest, identifier.id, and location fields, not the flat structure the scanner was expecting. This caused all extensions to appear as 'unknown' with empty names/publishers.\n\nThe fix reads the nested ext.manifest first, falls back to flat ext for backward compatibility with older backend envelopes. Also maps: location (new) / extensionLocation (old), identifier.id (new) / identifier.value (old), and properly propagates targetPlatform, isValid, and validationMessages instead of hardcoded defaults.\n\nThis ensures @builtin/@installed extension split works correctly in the sidebar.",
			"SHA": "b95789339ceca34e67c990698cbbae249e435711"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-04-23T01:19:28Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				215,
				3
			],
			"Message": "feat(Output): Separate system and user extension scanning in IPC shim\n\nThe ExtensionScannerIPC transform plugin now distinguishes between system and user extensions by calling the appropriate Mountain IPC methods:\n- `extensions:scanSystemExtensions` for built-in extensions (ForceBuiltin=true)\n- `extensions:scanUserExtensions` for user-installed extensions (ForceBuiltin=false)\n\nPreviously the scanner hardcoded `type: 0` and `isBuiltin: true` for all extensions, causing all extensions to appear under @builtin in the sidebar. Now it honours Mountain's ILocalExtension envelope values (type, isBuiltin, source) with ForceBuiltin as fallback for older envelopes.\n\nAlso implements the previously stubbed `scanUserExtensions()` method which was returning an empty array, and makes `scanAllUserExtensions()` delegate to it.",
			"SHA": "a7b92b8faec7cf334dc5f572fd889561f4703166"
		}
	],
	"TimeStamp": 1778683908652
}